cells:
- kind: 1
  value: '# Developer Productivity


    Database development includes a lot of work that surrounds the application logic itself: making deployment scripts safe
    to rerun, discovering database metadata from tools, and keeping client-side caches aware of schema changes.


    For a database developer, that work can become a collection of dictionary queries, exception handlers, polling loops,
    and client-specific plumbing. The useful question is whether the database can carry more of that responsibility directly.


    The capabilities in this story reduce that incidental work in three different places.


    **We will:**

    1. make repeatable DDL express its intent directly with `IF NOT EXISTS`;

    2. retrieve structured object metadata through `DBMS_DEVELOPER.GET_METADATA`;

    3. see the database-side change that would trigger Table DDL Change Notification for an OCI application.

    **Session boundary:** the notification subscriber and callback are application/OCI work. This notebook intentionally does not implement them.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '## 1. Make DDL deployment scripts naturally repeatable


    **Why this matters:**


    Database developers write setup and migration scripts that may be executed more than once. A developer rebuilds an environment,
    a CI/CD job retries a deployment, or a migration is applied to a database that already contains part of the target state.


    Before `IF NOT EXISTS`, the script had to work around the normal "object already exists" error. A common pattern was to
    query the data dictionary first and then create the object only when it was absent, or to use PL/SQL exception handling
    around dynamic DDL.


    That is deployment plumbing rather than the schema change itself.


    **What changes for development:**

    - the DDL states directly that an existing object is acceptable;

    - deployment scripts become easier to read and rerun;

    - less procedural existence-checking code is needed;

    - the intent is visible in the DDL instead of being hidden in control flow.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '**Before:** check the data dictionary and only execute the DDL when the object is not already present. The example
    uses PL/SQL because that was a common way to make repeatable scripts behave predictably.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: "DECLARE\n  l_exists NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n  SELECT COUNT(*)\n  INTO   l_exists\n  FROM   user_tables\n  WHERE  table_name\
    \ = 'DEV_PRODUCTIVITY_BEFORE';\n\n  IF l_exists = 0 THEN\n    EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'CREATE TABLE dev_productivity_before\
    \ (\n      id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,\n      value VARCHAR2(100)\n    )';\n  END IF;\nEND;\n/"
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: The developer has had to maintain both the existence test and the DDL. The database already knows whether the object
    exists, but the application script has to reproduce that decision logic.
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '**Now:** state the same intent in the DDL. The statement creates the table when it is absent and is a no-op when
    it is already present. Run this cell again to see that the same deployment statement can be rerun without adding procedural
    logic.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dev_productivity_now (\n  id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,\n  value VARCHAR2(100)\n);"
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: 'The business result is the same: the table exists. The development change is the amount of code that surrounds the
    schema change.


    Oracle documents `IF NOT EXISTS` as a no-op when the object already exists. The feature is available for supported DDL
    statements; this example uses `CREATE TABLE`.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '## 2. Retrieve database object metadata through a developer-oriented API


    **Why this matters:**


    Schema-aware development tools and applications regularly ask questions such as: What kind of object is this? What columns
    does it expose? What data types and structural properties does it have? A tool can answer those questions by querying
    dictionary views, but then each tool has to know which views and columns to use and how to combine them.


    That approach is especially repetitive when several tools need roughly the same metadata or when an application wants
    to cache the metadata in a predictable JSON form.


    **What changes for development:**

    - use a supported database API for common metadata retrieval;

    - receive structured metadata as JSON rather than assembling it from several dictionary queries;

    - make schema-aware tooling less dependent on internal dictionary-query details;

    - use the returned `ETAG` to determine whether a cached metadata document is still current.


    **Target developer:** this is most relevant to developers of IDE/database-browser tooling, schema introspection services,
    code generators, migration utilities, and applications that need to understand database objects dynamically. It is less
    important for an application developer who only queries known tables directly.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '**Before:** a tool composes the metadata it needs from dictionary views. Even a simple table description requires
    the tool to know which dictionary information to retrieve and how to shape the result.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: "SELECT table_name,\n       column_name,\n       data_type,\n       data_length,\n       nullable\nFROM   user_tab_columns\n\
    WHERE  table_name = 'DEV_PRODUCTIVITY_NOW'\nORDER BY column_id;"
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: '**Now:** `DBMS_DEVELOPER.GET_METADATA` returns a JSON-formatted metadata document for the object. The API supports
    `BASIC`, `TYPICAL`, and `ALL` levels so a tool can request only the amount of information it needs.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: "SELECT JSON_SERIALIZE(\n         DBMS_DEVELOPER.GET_METADATA(\n           name        => 'DEV_PRODUCTIVITY_NOW',\n\
    \           schema      => USER,\n           object_type => 'TABLE',\n           level       => 'BASIC'\n         ) RETURNING\
    \ CLOB PRETTY\n       ) AS object_metadata\nFROM dual;"
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: 'The developer outcome is a simpler integration boundary: the database provides a structured metadata contract and
    the consuming tool does not have to reproduce the same dictionary-query logic everywhere.


    **ETAG and change detection:** the metadata document includes an `ETAG`. A metadata-aware client can retain that value
    and provide it on a later request; when the current ETAG matches, the API can return an empty document, allowing the client
    to avoid processing unchanged metadata.


    **Team collaboration:** the database developer or DBA may need to grant access when describing objects outside the caller''s
    own schema. The tool/application team owns the client-side consumption of the returned JSON.


    **Programming languages:** `DBMS_DEVELOPER` is a PL/SQL package exposed by the database. Applications in Java, Python,
    C#, JavaScript/Node.js, C/C++, or other Oracle-supported client languages consume it through their Oracle database driver/API;
    there is no separate language-specific `DBMS_DEVELOPER` client library.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '## 3. Be aware of event-driven schema-change notification


    **Why this matters:**


    Some applications and middleware cache table metadata because repeatedly querying the database for the same metadata is
    wasteful. When a table changes, the cache has to be invalidated or refreshed.


    A polling design asks the database repeatedly whether anything changed. Most polls find that nothing changed, yet each
    one still consumes a request and a response.


    Oracle AI Database 26ai adds **Table DDL Change Notification** for OCI clients. An OCI application can register for table-
    or schema-level DDL notifications and receive an asynchronous event when supported table DDL commits.


    **What changes for development:**

    - replace a metadata polling loop with an event-driven subscription;

    - invalidate or refresh a metadata cache when the database reports a change;

    - reduce network round trips used only to discover that nothing changed;

    - keep the event-handling logic in the client while the database supplies the change signal.


    **Target developer:** this is most relevant to developers of middleware, database-aware services, schema registries, replication/integration
    components, and other applications that cache or interpret schema metadata. It is not a SQL/PLSQL feature; the notification
    client is implemented with OCI.


    **Team collaboration:** the application/platform team owns the OCI subscriber and callback; the database/DBA team must
    provide the required privileges and listener configuration. OCI DDL notification requires a non-SYS user with `SELECT
    ANY TABLE` and `CHANGE NOTIFICATION`, plus the documented TCP or IPC listener setup.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '**Database-side change:** make one committed DDL change to the watched table. The OCI client subscription is what
    receives the event.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: 'ALTER TABLE dev_productivity_now

    ADD description VARCHAR2(200);'
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: '**OCI client side:** register the table with `OCIDdlEventRegister` and a DDL notification subscription using `OCI_SUBSCR_CQ_QOS_DDL_NTFN`.
    The client callback receives information about the DDL event, including the operation type, table/object name and commit
    SCN.


    This is intentionally the end of the database demo. The OCI subscription/callback is application-side work and is not implemented here. The important DB developer takeaway is to know that Oracle Database 26ai can provide the signal and that the database/platform team owns the required preparation.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '## What changes for the database developer?


    **`IF NOT EXISTS`** removes routine object-existence handling from repeatable DDL.


    **`DBMS_DEVELOPER.GET_METADATA`** provides a supported metadata interface for tools and applications instead of requiring
    every client to reconstruct dictionary queries.


    **Table DDL Change Notification** lets OCI applications react to schema changes rather than repeatedly polling for them.


    The common outcome is reduced incidental development work: less deployment control flow, less metadata-query duplication,
    and fewer polling loops in applications that need to remain schema-aware.'
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 1
  value: '## References


    - `IF [NOT] EXISTS`: https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/adfns/development-guide.pdf

    - `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`: https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/sqlrf/CREATE-TABLE.html

    - `DBMS_DEVELOPER.GET_METADATA`: https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/arpls/database-pl-sql-packages-and-types-reference.pdf

    - Table DDL Change Notification: https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/adfns/using-table-ddl-change-notification.html

    - Changes in This Release for Oracle AI Database Development Guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/adfns/changes-this-release-oracle-database-development-guide.html'
  languageId: markdown
